The physically constrained fit now reaches Rwp 5.47% with 87.5 wt% ZnWO₄ and 12.5 wt% WO₃; ZnO remains unsupported. One warning emerged: unconstrained oxygen refinement can lower Rwp further but moves WO₃ oxygen sites far beyond the generator’s permitted reference mismatch, so I’m rejecting that overfit and testing the better-resolved equivalent WO₃ literature model instead.

The equivalent modern monoclinic WO₃ model fits better with chemically restrained W coordinates than the older model without requiring large oxygen shifts. I’m also checking the low-temperature triclinic WO₃ polymorph because its subtle high-angle splittings are the main remaining crystallographic ambiguity; this is the last inventory/model branch before finalization.

The triclinic branch is computationally heavier because it has four independent W and twelve O sites, but it is converging. I’m keeping its WO₃ coordinates fixed to the published model during this comparison, so any apparent gain must come from genuine peak splitting rather than extra coordinate freedom.

The phase assignment is now narrowed to textured wolframite ZnWO₄ plus monoclinic WO₃; ZnO is not supported. I’m checking the stricter coordinate-constrained refinement, then I’ll export the final CIFs/profile/project and replay-validate their consistency.

The stricter model retains essentially the same fit (Rwp 5.15%) while keeping the WO₃ coordinate shifts within 0.032 Å for W and 0.043 Å RMS for O; both structures pass the geometry/occupancy screen. I’m using this more conservative refinement for the submission.

I found and corrected a GSAS-II scripting issue in the bounded-coordinate stage: GSAS-II’s `dAx/dAy/dAz` variables are coordinate shifts, so their limits must be centered on zero. The earlier bounds were centered on absolute coordinates, creating frozen shifts and a saved-array/replay mismatch. I’m rerunning that stage correctly before final export.

A source-level check showed GSAS-II special-cases coordinate limits: although `dA` is an internal shift, the bounds are deliberately tested against the resulting absolute coordinate. I restored the original bound handling and discarded that diagnostic run; the conservative model itself remains valid. The remaining replay difference came from leaving bounded coordinate variables frozen, so I’m freezing the accepted coordinates and re-refining the profile terms for a self-consistent final project.

